# Brand Context

> Per-website file. The engine's writing/rewrite/brief/outline skills auto-load this.
> Fill every section. Keep it honest and specific — vague brand files produce vague blogs.

## Audience
- **Primary**: <who they are, expertise level, emotional state, what problem they have>
- **Secondary**: <secondary segment / geography>
- **Active problems**: <the concrete situations they search for>
- **Common misconceptions**: <what they wrongly believe, so the content can correct it>

## Positioning
- **Official entity name**: <Brand>
- **Homepage**: <https://...>
- **Logo**: <https://.../logo.png>
- **sameAs profiles**: <social/profile URLs — keep the handle consistent across all>
- **Mission**: <one sentence>
- **Distinctive POV**: <the opinion that makes this brand different>
- **What we are NOT**: <anti-positioning — the shortcuts/claims we refuse>
- **Competitors**: <name — how we differ>

## Editorial Rules
### Always do
- <e.g. open answer-first with a ~50-60 word Quick answer a snippet can lift>
- <e.g. cite primary sources over secondary blogs>
- <e.g. include exactly one honest admission of limitation per article>

### Never do
- <e.g. promise guaranteed outcomes / fixed timelines>
- <e.g. invent statistics, case studies, client names>
- <e.g. use em dashes>

### Taboo phrases
- <banned marketing/AI words and phrases, comma-separated>

### Required disclosures
- <e.g. "This is general information, not legal advice." on legal topics>

## Topic Scope
- **In scope (core pillars)**: <the 4-8 pillar topics>
- **Partial scope**: <edge topics allowed only with a specific angle>
- **Out of scope**: <what to refuse>
